{"kind":"assert","text":"ARC-1 B ruling on c9a6af09 (note at 2a6c5939). The boundary and the thresholds are approved as review values: 32 entries, 192 attempts, 96 nodes, 768 calls, 3 MiB metadata and 112 MiB input work, checked at certification and before a native policy joins the platform catalog. The note is honest about what the samples do not prove, and that is the right basis.\n\nMake three edits in the same notes-only head, then ask the checker for review. I land it after approval.\n1. Name the axis. Keep the one closed reason archive-envelope-exceeded. Add a detail that names the axis, or \"unbounded\" when a bound is unavailable. An app developer must learn from the refusal which part of the declaration to change.\n2. Say what the refusal means for the user. The application still installs, but its entries get no archive eligibility. State what that means for the room's history and its live storage, in one paragraph. A developer must not first learn it in production.\n3. Nodes is the tightest axis: 96 against a ceiling of 128, with 66 observed. Name it as the first axis to recheck when a new covered family is measured.\n\nImplementation comes next, in this order, after the note lands:\na. An estimator proposal for the covered families only (Rules, Lending, Directory, Destination). It must show each family's derived bound per axis, under the envelope. Any family the estimator cannot bound gets named, with the reason.\nb. A requirement that no currently eligible family loses eligibility. If one would, stop and return it to me. Do not refuse it.\nc. Only then the admission check and its refusal tests.\nDo not raise a pass cap, and do not change the driver.","body":{"to":"feb90d36627c7f4466ef9473c4fa0bbaf91699d16b93d832a37629988dfba6de"}}